Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.039
I've got a pop quiz for you.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.229
What database system has been around

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.379
for almost 50 years?

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.939
I guess you can guessed it by the

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.499
topic of this video.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.869
It's a relational database.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:13.870
Hello, my name is Jamil Spain,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.889
Developer Advocate with the IBM

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.539
Cloud and Brand Technical

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.099
Specialists in the U.S.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.079
Financial Services market.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.669
And today's talk, we're going to

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.919
dive a little bit into relational

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.789
databases.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.779
I'm going to get into some of the

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.969
characteristics of the database

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.099
model and also some benefits

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.259
that it provides for you when making

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.999
your decision that you want to use

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.249
this for your next project

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.209
to get started.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.099
Let's talk about the structure

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.989
of a relational database

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.039
system.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.199
It's going to pretty much map

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.479
out your data points

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.369
and bring out the relationships

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.749
in that. And the way that it

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.519
accomplishes that is through.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.559
The first thing is making tables,

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.929
which represents particular

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.909
items or nouns or particular

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.739
entities of your particular

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.509
system that you want to actually

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.339
prototype. So let's say I

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.360
have a bunch of books.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.549
Now this is going to be a table.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.679
It accomplishes the attributes

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.649
of these books by having columns

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.179
and columns will be things like

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.180
name.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.520
Date.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.050
OK, maybe author.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.979
Of course, got to have a author for

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.540
a book and every.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.769
Table is going to have an ID, feel

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.899
some way to uniquely identify

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.209
it. Each each row

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.210
of this.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.289
Database table represents an

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.129
actual record or an item,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.289
a type of book.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.689
That's how it really fulfills

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.669
that ability to hold the database

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.670
system there.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.679
And then as you wait, you

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.959
kind of query out this particular

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.989
system is through what we call a

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.209
you will a structured query

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:02.619
language.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.999
And it's a particular system, you

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.469
can say select.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.619
All the books.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.139
And give me all this data out

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.699
for me to use.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.919
Now we have one particular item

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.809
here books, but where it really

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.429
comes, you really see the power of a

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.839
relational database.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.089
Let's say now I want to

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.259
show the authors.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:31.479
And the way that relational

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.609
databases can really provide

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.199
the structure of relating these two

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.179
entities together is by

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.189
describing them through a

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.529
particular entity or relationships.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.119
You have one that you're here about

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.189
where books can have multiple

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.779
authors, so maybe a one.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.419
To many.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.529
You can have a one

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:54.669
to one.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.479
And just a couple of relationships

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.489
you can you can kind of mirror

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.349
within the database system, so one

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.189
way that I can have this kind of a

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.549
normalized structure is to say

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.379
definitely the authors

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.380
is going to have.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.459
Its own particular ID

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.529
and name any, any any

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.209
any particular order of attributes

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.459
that I want to describe there,

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.709
but I can also one of these fields

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.289
can be an actual number

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.180
that actually relates to the ID.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.719
Of this feel, and this is what's

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.129
known as a foreign key.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.679
And of course, there will be a name

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.329
there, so when I do my query, the

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.339
next query I do, I

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.229
can reference both these tables

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.479
and say, Hey, give me all the books

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.859
and give me more of the author

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.169
information from this table using

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.029
this foreign key to

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.189
describe that particular structure

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.089
there.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.439
And you can imagine you'll be

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.879
running a lot of these queries a

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.979
lot, and there are some benefits

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.419
or some things that are provided to

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.279
help with those to speed

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.229
up these queries and make them

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.959
operate as efficiently as possible.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.489
You have the database will provide

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.779
ways that you can kind of set an

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.669
index. Let's say that I'm often

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.619
querying and always trying to pull

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.449
out by name or by date.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.830
I can set an index.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.119
On this particular column,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.189
which will automatically index all

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:17.929
the values, so the next time you

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.549
run, this query is going to pull

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.559
from what we like to call this cache

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.029
of all the values.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.859
Most importantly, every

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.799
time new roles are added,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.049
records are added to this particular

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.389
table. The index keeps building

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.219
this recent, I like to say,

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:35.929
cache of all these values, so it's

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.999
always keeping up to date

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.289
to work there.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:40.999
So this is all the characteristics

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.039
of what you kind of have to model

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.989
out your particular

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:46.459
application that you want to build

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.379
and describe how the entities

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.479
are related to each other.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:51.949
Now let's switch over here to some

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.569
of the benefits that you're going to

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.499
get out of the box with

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.199
a relational database

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.459
that you want to use.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.279
The first we'll talk about

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.529
is actual

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:04.530
consistency.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.729
Now, this is useful because let's

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.259
say there is some point where I want

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.699
to run multiple of these

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.599
transactions.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.219
Let's say I'm modeling an inventory

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.779
system for an E commerce

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.849
application, and I have the need

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.799
to write several

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.809
queries in a row that may

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.639
manage a couple of

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.139
different subsystems.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.479
But if one of those queries fails,

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.489
I want the option to be able

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.589
to roll back.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:38.859
The whole set of all

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.089
those queries, just to make sure

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.729
that my data always maintains

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.679
some consistency there.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.629
The second is the option

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:50.539
to do stored procedures.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.519
Sounds like a very complicated

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:53.599
word there.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.029
But think of store procedures as

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.189
the ability to write multiple blocks

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:00.169
or functions of code,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.309
or if there are a certain amount of

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.049
queries that I continue to always

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.239
run, I can kind of model

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.099
these. So it takes me less

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.869
code to write that particular

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.959
to access that same functionality

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.369
with less SQL code.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.289
And as you start to get into more

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.599
SQL writing with

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:19.639
relational databases, you'll see

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.469
queries can get pretty big.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.329
Another way

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:24.739
you can do this, as well as another

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.479
topic code views here.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.649
Definitely want to introduce some of

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:29.539
these keywords as you do your deeper

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.399
dove. You were able to see a lot

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.379
of these and it'd be familiar

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.189
to you.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.919
And the last one is a mechanism

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:38.779
to handle locking

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:39.780
in the database.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:42.470
And concurrency.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.279
Why is that important?

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.429
Well, at some

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.379
point multiple users are going

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.999
to want to use the database, maybe

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.709
at the same time, maybe two

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.149
applications are going to be adding

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:00.049
in books.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:02.149
And if the first application

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.039
is making a particular

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.629
insert into this table.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.829
You're going to want to make sure

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.839
that the database is locked until

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.669
that right is done and then

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.679
the second application has its

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.539
ability to go in and make its insert

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:16.349
as well.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.839
So these are all built into

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.519
a lot of the relational databases

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.479
that you use.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.159
A lot of these benefits will be

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:24.989
provided for you out of the box, not

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.179
necessarily something you'll have to

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.129
write in code, but just know

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.619
that you'll have this at top of mind

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.839
when you are kind of selecting

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.579
to use a relational database for

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.289
your next project.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.579
I hope this is good for you to

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.469
understand the foundation of

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.389
relational databases.

